---
// Article for the Glitch Text page (en). Written by hand; the tool above it comes from GlitchPage.
import GlitchPage from '../components/GlitchPage.astro';
---

<GlitchPage lang="en">
  <article class="wrap max-w-[900px] mx-auto py-10 text-text">
    <!-- Intro -->
    <header class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-[clamp(1.6rem,3.4vw,2.2rem)] font-bold mb-4 text-center">Copy and Paste Glitch Text</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.8]">Glitch text can be used in comments on blogs and social media platforms such as Facebook, YouTube, and Tumblr. It uses Unicode characters instead of images, so glitchy marks remain part of the text when copied and pasted. For example, ļ̵͝i̴͋ͅk̵̡̚e̵̝̎ ̷̡́ẗ̸̠h̵͚̊i̸͈̐s̴̬̚ is a form of distorted text created with combining characters. However, some websites accept only certain Unicode characters, which may cause glitchy marks to disappear. Email addresses and website domains generally require standard characters.</p>
    </header>

    <!-- How to type -->
    <section class="mb-12 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">How to Type Weird Glitch Text?</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-[1.8]">Weird glitch text is created by adding special Unicode characters called combining characters to ordinary letters. These characters attach visual marks to existing letters, creating a distorted appearance without changing the original letters. Diacritics, which normally indicate pronunciation or distinguish letters in written languages, can also create creepy text when several marks are combined. A text translator or glitch text generator automates this process, allowing users to generate Zalgo text without inserting Unicode symbols manually.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Zalgo -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">What Is Zalgo?</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-[1.8] mb-4">Zalgo is an internet meme that began with Dave Kelly's edits of comic strips in 2004. These edits showed characters being overtaken by a dark force. Zalgo is associated with the fictional entity known as "He Who Waits Behind the Wall" and the Nezperdian Hivemind.</p>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-[1.8]">Zalgo text uses multiple combining characters to distort ordinary letters. The marks can extend above, below, and through the text, creating its characteristic creepy appearance.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- How it works -->
    <section class="mb-12 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">How Does Glitch Text Work?</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-[1.8] mb-4">Glitch text works through Unicode combining characters. A combining character is a Unicode code point that attaches to a preceding base character rather than appearing as an independent letter. Multiple combining characters can follow a single letter, causing their marks to stack or overlap.</p>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-[1.8]">The Unicode Combining Diacritical Marks block contains characters from U+0300 to U+036F. Glitch text generators use these and other combining characters to produce different text distortions.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Types -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-2">Types of Glitch Text</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-[1.8] mb-6">Combining characters can create three common visual effects:</p>
      <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 flex flex-col gap-3">
        <li class="card px-5 py-3.5 text-sm leading-[1.7]"><strong class="font-semibold">Above:</strong> <span class="text-text-muted">Marks appear above letters, extending the text upward.</span></li>
        <li class="card px-5 py-3.5 text-sm leading-[1.7]"><strong class="font-semibold">Through:</strong> <span class="text-text-muted">Marks cross letters, creating a scratched or corrupted appearance.</span></li>
        <li class="card px-5 py-3.5 text-sm leading-[1.7]"><strong class="font-semibold">Below:</strong> <span class="text-text-muted">Marks appear underneath letters, extending the text downward.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-[1.8] mt-6">These effects can be combined to produce heavily distorted Zalgo text.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Safe -->
    <section class="mb-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Is Glitch Text Safe to Use?</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-[1.8]">Glitch text uses Unicode characters and is generally harmless. However, excessive combining characters can make text difficult to read, overlap nearby lines, and interfere with screen readers. Some websites and applications may also remove unsupported characters or display them incorrectly.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</GlitchPage>
